    slug: "qatif-tarout-island-heritage-guide-2026",
    title: "Qatif & Tarout Island Heritage Guide 2026 — Eastern Province Old Town",
    excerpt: "Everything about Qatif old town and Tarout Island — the Dilmun ruins, Tarout Castle, date palm groves, the fish market, and how to visit from Dammam or Khobar.",
    content: `<h2>Qatif — Arabia&apos;s Ancient Gulf Port</h2><p>Qatif is one of the oldest continuously inhabited settlements on the Arabian Gulf — a port city with documented history stretching back 5,000 years. Today it is largely an ordinary Saudi city of 500,000 people, but within it survives Al-Qadimah: the old walled town with traditional mud-brick architecture, narrow covered alleys, and a historic souq that predates oil. 25km north of Dammam, Qatif is an easy half-day trip from the Eastern Province cities.</p><h2>Tarout Island — Dilmun Civilisation</h2><p>Five kilometres north of Qatif (connected by short causeway), Tarout Island is one of the most archaeologically significant sites in Arabia. The island was a major port and centre of the Dilmun civilisation (3000–800 BCE) — the ancient Gulf civilisation that features in Sumerian texts as the land of paradise. Tarout Castle (Al-Qal&apos;a) — the ruined fort on the island&apos;s highest point — dates to the Portuguese occupation (1521–1602 CE) but is built on earlier Dilmun foundations. The island has a traditional fishing village atmosphere and a well-known fish market.</p><h2>Qatif Old Town (Al-Qadimah)</h2><p>The historic walled district of Qatif survives in fragmented but substantial form. The main gateway and covered souq area are the most accessible entry point. Traditional buildings — some still inhabited, others preserved as heritage sites — line the narrow lanes. The covered market has local produce, spices, and traditional goods. The most authentic old-city experience in the Eastern Province.</p><h2>Khalas Dates</h2><p>Qatif is famous throughout Saudi Arabia for its Khalas dates — honey-coloured, soft, and intensely sweet. Considered among the finest dates in the Kingdom. The Qatif oasis date palm groves have been cultivated for centuries. Fresh Khalas dates are sold at roadside stalls from August to September (harvest season). Outside harvest season, packed Khalas are available at Qatif souqs year-round.</p><h2>Getting to Qatif from Dammam</h2><p>Qatif is 25km north of Dammam — approximately 30 minutes by taxi. From Al Khobar: 55km, 50 minutes via the Dammam-Khobar highway. A day trip from Dammam or Khobar is very feasible: Qatif old town (1 hour), Tarout Island (1 hour), fish market (30 minutes), and Corniche lunch — half day comfortably.</p>`,
